<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hamid Ali Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1875">1875</a>–<a href="/years/1930">1930</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Hamid Ali Khan Bahadur</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hamid Ali Khan was Nawab of Rampur from 1889 until his death in 1930. A patron of learning, music and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> poetry, he enlarged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raza-library">Rampur Raza Library</a> and built the Hamid Manzil palace that now houses it. His court attracted poets and musicians, continuing Rampur's role as a refuge for the culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hamid-ali-khan-of-rampur.jpg" alt="Hamid Ali Khan" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vandyk photographic studio · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir-Sayed-Mohammad-Hamid-Ali-Khan-Bahadur-Nawab-of-Rampur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
